import{_ as o,o as t,c as a,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Jobs and configuration","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/other.md","filePath":"components/other.md"}'),d={name:"components/other.md"};function c(i,e,r,n,l,h){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="jobs-and-configuration" tabindex="-1">Jobs and configuration <a class="header-anchor" href="#jobs-and-configuration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Jobs and configuration&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page describes the components that are neither agents, skills, hooks, scripts nor adapters: the background jobs, an agent workflow, the configuration files and the test queries of the skills. Most of them reach the user through a copy that the user makes once. Of the files of this page, <code>harness install</code> copies only <code>Project.toml</code>. <code>harness render</code> prints a template of this page with the values of the profile.</p><p>The sections follow the purpose of the files:</p><ul><li>the launch agents of macOS, in <code>launchagents/</code>, and their <code>systemd --user</code> units for Linux, in <code>systemd/</code>;</li><li>the agent workflow <code>agent-workflows/repo-drift.js</code>;</li><li>the settings template, the example profile and the example model tables;</li><li>the Julia environment of the scripts, <code>Project.toml</code>;</li><li>the linter configuration: <code>fatou.toml</code> and the files of <code>ast-grep/</code>;</li><li>the leak check, <code>.gitleaks.toml</code>;</li><li>the test queries of the skills, <code>tests/skill-triggers/</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="claude-autocommit-and-claude-autocommit-plist" tabindex="-1"><code>claude-autocommit</code> and <code>claude-autocommit.plist</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#claude-autocommit-and-claude-autocommit-plist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`claude-autocommit` and `claude-autocommit.plist`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A macOS launch agent is a job that launchd, the service manager of macOS, runs for one user: on a schedule, at login, or as a server that it keeps alive. Each job is a plist file in <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/</code>. The plists in <code>launchagents/</code> are templates: their label starts with the profile key <code>launchd_prefix</code>, and their paths hold <code>{home}</code>. The harness has no subcommand that installs them. <code>harness render launchagents/&lt;name&gt;</code> prints the rendered plist.</p><p><code>claude-autocommit</code> is a Python program that commits the project memories of <code>~/.claude/</code>, which must be a git repository. It commits <code>projects/*/memory/</code> only, and pushes the local <code>main</code> to the branch of <code>origin</code> that <code>CLAUDE_AUTOCOMMIT_BRANCH</code> names, <code>main</code> when the variable is unset. The plist and the unit set it from the profile key <code>autocommit_branch</code>: <code>main</code> on the template host, and a branch of its own on each other machine. The template host is the machine whose Claude Code memory is the template for the other machines. Without the key <code>autocommit_branch</code>, the profile key <code>template_host</code> gives the branch: <a href="./../profile#one-profile-for-every-machine">One profile for every machine</a>. The script stages each path by name and commits with a pathspec, so a file that another session staged stays staged. It skips a file that changed in the last 5 minutes, and refuses a rename or more than 20 deletions.</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>claude-autocommit [--dry-run] [--no-push] [--help]</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>claude-autocommit.plist</code> runs the installed copy <code>~/.local/bin/claude-autocommit</code> at load and every 15 minutes, on the interpreter of the harness&#39;s Python environment, <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/python/bin/python</code>. The script imports only the standard library. Edit the script here, then copy it there again.</p><p>The script runs on macOS and on Linux. A lock directory stops a second run, and the script removes it on exit and on a SIGTERM. A SIGTERM goes on to the running git, which removes its own <code>index.lock</code>, and the run ends after that git. A git command that takes longer than 10 minutes also gets a SIGTERM, and the run exits 1 with one <code>ERROR:</code> line. On macOS the lock is <code>~/Library/Logs/claude-autocommit/lock</code>, beside the job&#39;s log. On Linux it is <code>$XDG_STATE_HOME/claude-autocommit/lock</code>, or <code>~/.local/state/claude-autocommit/lock</code> when <code>XDG_STATE_HOME</code> is unset or empty. The cases of <code>lib/harness/autocommit.py</code> in <code>harness test</code> run the script on fixture repositories with a bare remote, on the system that runs them, and check the commit, the branch that receives the push, the lock and the refusals.</p><h2 id="julia-update-plist" tabindex="-1"><code>julia-update.plist</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#julia-update-plist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`julia-update.plist`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A launch agent that keeps the Julia installation and the Kaimon server current. It runs daily at 04:30, and not at login. The job runs <code>~/.local/bin/julia-update.jl</code>, the installed copy of <code>scripts/julia-update.jl</code>, with the juliaup launcher <code>~/.juliaup/bin/julia</code>, under the Seatbelt profile <code>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/julia-update.sb</code>, as <a href="#kaimon-plist"><code>kaimon.plist</code></a> runs under its own. <a href="./scripts">The scripts</a> describes what the script does.</p><p>The job runs the installed copy and not the copy in <code>~/Research</code>, because a session can write <code>~/Research</code> and the job runs outside the Claude Code sandbox. It sets <code>JULIA_PKG_USE_CLI_GIT=true</code>, so Pkg uses the <code>git</code> program, and a <code>PATH</code> for that <code>git</code>. It runs at a low priority, so it does not compete with an interactive Julia session. Its output goes to <code>~/Library/Logs/julia-update/</code>. On Linux, the script runs as the <code>systemd --user</code> unit <code>julia-update.service</code>, whose output goes to the journal: <code>journalctl --user -u julia-update.service</code>.</p><p>The label is <code>&lt;launchd_prefix&gt;.julia-update</code>. Render the plist with <code>harness render launchagents/julia-update.plist</code>. <code>harness leaks</code> renders it with the example profile.</p><h2 id="kaimon-plist" tabindex="-1"><code>kaimon.plist</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#kaimon-plist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`kaimon.plist`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A launch agent that runs the Kaimon MCP server. Kaimon gives an agent a Julia session and tools for Julia code. The job starts at login, and launchd restarts it when it exits. It runs <code>~/.julia/bin/kaimon --headless --port 2828</code> under the Seatbelt profile <code>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/kaimon.sb</code>, with <code>/usr/bin/sandbox-exec -f</code>; <code>harness setup</code> renders the profile from <code>sandbox/daemon.toml</code> (<a href="./../harness-command#jobsandbox-py"><code>jobsandbox.py</code></a>). The first directory of its <code>PATH</code>, <code>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/shims</code>, holds the <code>ps</code> that <code>harness setup</code> installs from <code>sandbox/shims/</code>: the exec of the setuid <code>/bin/ps</code> fails under the profile, and the shim answers Kaimon&#39;s check of a session&#39;s process with <code>kill -0</code>.</p><p>The working directory is <code>~/Research</code>. Do not remove it: Kaimon reads its tool policy from <code>.kaimon/tools.json</code> in its working directory, and without it Kaimon offers every default tool. A restart waits at least 60 s, so a second server that cannot use the port does not restart many times a second. <code>JULIA_APPS_JULIA_CMD</code> names the juliaup launcher, so the job does not depend on one Julia release. <code>KAIMON_QDRANT_MANAGED=off</code> stops the background index. <code>KAIMON_GATE_PROMOTE_AFTER=240</code> lets an <code>ex</code> call wait up to 240 s for its result before Kaimon makes it a background job; Kaimon&#39;s own default is 30 s. An agent that gets a job id has no way to wait, so it calls <code>check_eval</code> until the job ends, and each call is a model turn that reads the whole context again. 240 s is below the 5-minute prompt cache of a Claude Code sub-agent and below the request timeout that the install gives oh-my-pi. Claude Code, OpenCode and Antigravity keep a Kaimon call open for longer than that. <code>POCL_CACHE_DIR</code> gives PoCL, which runs the kernels of the CPU backend of KernelAbstractions, the kernel cache <code>~/.cache/kaimon/pocl</code> in a Julia session. Kaimon&#39;s profile lets the job write below <code>~/.cache/kaimon</code> and not PoCL&#39;s default <code>~/.cache/pocl</code>, which a sandboxed command may write: so Kaimon has a cache of its own, and no command can put a compiled kernel where Kaimon loads and runs it. <code>harness setup</code> creates the directory before it loads the job, each directory that it creates with mode 700. <code>JULIA_PKG_OFFLINE=true</code> starts Pkg offline in the server and in every session: the profile lets the job connect only to the loopback addresses and to Kaimon&#39;s own sockets below <code>~/.cache/kaimon</code> (<a href="./../safeguards/sandbox#the-daemon-profiles">The daemon profiles</a>), so a session&#39;s own <code>julia</code> or the update job installs a package that the depot lacks.</p><p>OpenCode, oh-my-pi and Antigravity connect to this server at <code>http://127.0.0.1:2828/</code>. The update job <code>julia-update.plist</code> restarts it when its Julia release is deleted. Render the plist with <code>harness render launchagents/kaimon.plist</code>.</p><h2 id="kaimon-service-julia-update-service-julia-update-timer-claude-autocommit-service-and-claude-autocommit-timer" tabindex="-1"><code>kaimon.service</code>, <code>julia-update.service</code>, <code>julia-update.timer</code>, <code>claude-autocommit.service</code> and <code>claude-autocommit.timer</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#kaimon-service-julia-update-service-julia-update-timer-claude-autocommit-service-and-claude-autocommit-timer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`kaimon.service`, `julia-update.service`, `julia-update.timer`, `claude-autocommit.service` and `claude-autocommit.timer`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <code>systemd --user</code> units in <code>systemd/</code> run the three jobs of the plists on Linux. Like the plists, they are templates of the profile: <code>harness render systemd/&lt;name&gt;</code> prints a rendered unit, and <code>harness setup</code> installs them with what they need. <code>kaimon.service</code> and <code>julia-update.service</code> start their program through <code>~/.local/bin/jobbox</code> under <code>bwrap</code>, with the arguments of <code>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/&lt;job&gt;.bwrap</code> (<a href="./../harness-command#jobsandbox-py"><code>jobsandbox.py</code></a>). By hand, install <code>sandbox/jobbox</code> and <code>scripts/julia-update-server</code> into <code>~/.local/bin</code>, each with mode 755, write <code>harness render sandbox/&lt;job&gt;.bwrap</code> for <code>kaimon</code> and <code>julia-update</code> into <code>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/</code>, create each write deny and each pin of the two files, <code>~/.julia/juliaup/.juliaup-lock</code> and <code>~/.cache/julia-update</code>, copy each rendered unit into <code>~/.config/systemd/user/</code>, run <code>systemctl --user daemon-reload</code>, create the server&#39;s PoCL cache, and enable the server and the two timers:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>(umask 077 &amp;&amp; mkdir -p ~/.cache/kaimon/pocl)</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>systemctl --user enable --now kaimon.service julia-update.timer claude-autocommit.timer</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Each unit describes the same job as its plist:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">unit</th><th style="text-align:left;">plist</th><th style="text-align:left;">how it runs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>kaimon.service</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>kaimon.plist</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">at login (<code>WantedBy=default.target</code>), restarted on exit after 60 s (<code>Restart=always</code>, <code>RestartSec=60</code>), in <code>~/Research</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>julia-update.service</code>, <code>julia-update.timer</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>julia-update.plist</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">daily at 04:30 (<code>OnCalendar</code>), not at login</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>claude-autocommit.service</code>, <code>claude-autocommit.timer</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>claude-autocommit.plist</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">when the timer starts (<code>OnActiveSec=0</code>), then 15 minutes after each run (<code>OnUnitActiveSec</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The program and its arguments, the environment and the <code>PATH</code> are those of the plist, with <code>jobbox &lt;job&gt;</code> in place of <code>sandbox-exec -f &lt;profile&gt;</code>, and without the shim directory of <code>kaimon.plist</code>, which is for macOS only. <code>julia-update.service</code> runs <code>~/.local/bin/julia-update-server</code> outside <code>bwrap</code> as its <code>ExecStopPost</code>: the server check, the restart and the alerts of <a href="./scripts">the script</a>. Both timers set <code>Persistent=true</code>, so a calendar start that the machine missed while it slept runs when the timer starts again. The units are <code>--user</code> units only. Their output goes to the journal: <code>journalctl --user -u &lt;name&gt;</code>. <code>julia-update-server</code> restarts the server as <code>kaimon.service</code>.</p><p>The jobs run outside the sandbox, so their <code>PATH</code> holds no directory that a session can write. Each directory is one that the settings template keeps out of a session&#39;s writes, because no <code>allowWrite</code> entry covers it or a <code>denyWrite</code> entry names it, or one of <code>/usr/bin</code>, <code>/bin</code>, <code>/usr/sbin</code>, <code>/sbin</code>, <code>/usr/local/bin</code> and <code>/opt/homebrew/bin</code>. No directory is at or below <code>$TMPDIR</code> (also its real path), <code>/var/folders</code> or <code>/private/var/folders</code>, which the live sandbox lets a session write. <code>harness test</code> renders each unit and its plist with the example profile and checks all of this, through <code>units.py</code> of <a href="./../harness-command#units-py">the harness command</a>. CI renders each unit on <code>ubuntu-latest</code> and runs <code>systemd-analyze verify --user</code> on it. <code>harness leaks</code> renders them with the example profile.</p><h2 id="repo-drift-js" tabindex="-1"><code>repo-drift.js</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#repo-drift-js" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`repo-drift.js`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <code>Workflow</code> script of Claude Code that checks whether the repositories of the tree still hold the canonical copies of <code>githooks/</code>. <code>agent-workflows/README.md</code> explains the directory and its rules for a workflow script.</p><p>Call it with absolute paths of repositories below a <code>Research/</code> directory:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Workflow({scriptPath: &#39;&lt;home&gt;/Research/Harness/agent-workflows/repo-drift.js&#39;,</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>          args: [&#39;&lt;home&gt;/Research/Packages/&lt;name&gt;&#39;, ...]})</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The first stage runs one agent per repository, on a small model. It compares the two git hooks, the CI, dependabot, Documenter and TagBot files, and <code>codecov.yml</code> at the repository root with <code>Harness/githooks/</code>. It also reports <code>core.hooksPath</code> and whether <code>.gitignore</code> and <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> exist. A second stage reads each differing file again, and confirms or overturns the finding. The result names each repository that returned nothing. The script compares <code>TagBot.yml</code> in every repository, and <code>harness workflows</code> adds it only to a package, so its row &quot;missing&quot; in an experiment is expected.</p><p>The script only reads. Check its result against <code>githooks/verify-workflows.jl</code>. To correct a drift, run <code>harness workflows</code> or <code>harness githooks</code>.</p><h2 id="settings-proposal-json" tabindex="-1"><code>settings.proposal.json</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#settings-proposal-json" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`settings.proposal.json`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The settings template, the policy source of all four frontends. It holds the Claude Code settings with placeholders such as <code>{home}</code>, <code>{org}</code> and <code>{protected_dir}</code>, which the profile fills in.</p><p>The template has the sections <code>permissions</code>, <code>hooks</code>, <code>sandbox</code> and <code>modelSettings</code>, and other keys of <code>settings.json</code>. These components read it:</p><ul><li><code>harness install</code> merges <code>permissions</code>, <code>hooks</code>, <code>sandbox</code> and <code>modelSettings</code> into <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. It keeps every other key of that file, and adds each other key of the template that the file does not have;</li><li><code>harness settings surface</code>, <code>compare</code> and <code>twins</code> measure and check it;</li><li><code>harness permissions</code> writes the OpenCode permission block from its <code>ask</code> and <code>deny</code> rules;</li><li><code>harness install</code> writes the <code>bash.patterns</code> and the path list of oh-my-pi from it;</li><li><code>harness install</code> writes the permission rules of Antigravity&#39;s CLI, the grants of its desktop app and the path list of its guard hook from it;</li><li><code>harness leaks</code> renders it with the example profile.</li></ul><p>Edit the template, never the installed <code>settings.json</code>, and commit it when you install it. <a href="./../daily-use">Typical use</a> gives the steps. <a href="./../safeguards/security-model">The security model</a> says which of its mechanisms is a boundary and which only prevents accidents.</p><p>The template names no host: its <code>sandbox.network.allowedDomains</code> is empty, and it has no <code>WebFetch(domain:…)</code> rule. <code>network.toml</code> gives them.</p><h2 id="network-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>network.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#network-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`network.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The hosts of the network rules of Claude Code, in four arrays: <code>allow</code>, the hosts that the <code>WebFetch</code> tool and a sandboxed command can reach; <code>fetch</code>, the hosts that only the <code>WebFetch</code> tool can reach; <code>infrastructure</code>, the hosts that only a sandboxed command can reach; <code>deny</code>, the hosts that neither can reach, with a reason for each in <code>[deny_reasons]</code>. Comments group the hosts: literature, mathematics, Julia, GitHub, package registries.</p><p><code>harness install</code>, <code>harness render</code> of the settings template and <code>harness settings</code> generate the <code>WebFetch(domain:…)</code> rules and the lists of <code>sandbox.network</code> from this file and from the overlay <code>network.toml</code> beside the profile, which <code>examples/network.toml</code> shows. A <code>deny</code> entry of either file wins over an <code>allow</code>, <code>fetch</code> or <code>infrastructure</code> entry of either file. OpenCode and oh-my-pi do not read the file; for Antigravity, <code>harness install</code> writes the <code>allow</code> and <code>fetch</code> hosts as <code>read_url(&lt;host&gt;)</code> rules. <a href="./../profile#the-network-overlay">Adapting the profile</a> gives the rules of each key.</p><h2 id="profile-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>profile.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#profile-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`profile.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The example of the private profile, <code>examples/profile.toml</code>. The profile holds every value that names a person, an institution or a machine, for example the home directory, the GitHub accounts and the strings that must never be in the repository. A template names a key as <code>{key}</code>.</p><p>Copy the file to <code>~/.config/research-harness/profile.toml</code>, and write your values into the copy. <code>harness --profile F</code> and <code>$RESEARCH_HARNESS_PROFILE</code> name another path. The example has no <code>home</code> and writes its paths with <code>~/</code>, so it takes the home directory of the user who runs <code>harness</code>.</p><p>The harness uses the example too. <code>harness leaks</code> renders every template with it, and the test cases use it as their dummy profile. So it must name every key that a template uses. <a href="./../profile">Adapting the profile</a> names each key and the subcommands that read it.</p><h2 id="models-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>models.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#models-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`models.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The example of the model tables, <code>examples/models.toml</code>. An agent or a skill names a tier in its <code>model:</code>: <code>large</code>, <code>medium</code> or <code>small</code>. The model tables map each tier to a model of each frontend.</p><p><code>[claude]</code> gives the Claude Code model of each tier, and <code>[antigravity]</code> the Antigravity tier of each tier: <code>inherit</code>, <code>flash</code>, <code>flash_lite</code> or <code>pro</code>, because an Antigravity agent can name no model. OpenCode and oh-my-pi share every other table, so that their agents run on the same models: the model of each tier, a model for one agent, the variants, the reasoning effort, the councils, which copy an agent onto other models, and the context limits. <code>[opencode]</code> and <code>[omp]</code> hold the same sub-tables, and each of their keys replaces the shared key for that frontend alone. When a table names a tier by an old name, <code>opus</code>, <code>sonnet</code> or <code>haiku</code>, <code>harness install</code> stops with exit status 2.</p><p>Copy the file to <code>models.toml</code> beside the profile. <code>harness --models F</code> and <code>$RESEARCH_HARNESS_MODELS</code> name another path. Only <code>harness install</code> reads it. The test cases use the example as it is. <a href="./../profile">Adapting the profile</a> describes each table.</p><h2 id="providers-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>providers.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#providers-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`providers.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The example of the providers, <code>examples/providers.toml</code>: the model servers and accounts of OpenCode and oh-my-pi beyond their built-in providers, each written once. A table is one provider, with neutral keys such as <code>api</code>, <code>url</code> and <code>key</code>, and its models. <code>harness install</code> writes each provider into the <code>provider</code> object of OpenCode&#39;s <code>opencode.jsonc</code> and into oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>models.yml</code>.</p><p>Copy the file to <code>providers.toml</code> beside the profile, and write your providers into the copy. The file is optional. The test cases copy the example beside their scratch profile. <a href="./../profile#the-providers">Adapting the profile</a> maps each key to each frontend.</p><h2 id="repositories-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>repositories.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#repositories-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`repositories.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The example of the repository table, <code>examples/repositories.toml</code>: the repositories of the research tree and their clone URLs, for <code>harness clone</code> and <code>harness repos</code>. A top-level key is a fixed repository, <code>knowledge</code>, <code>environment</code>, <code>tasks</code> or <code>library</code>; a table is a group, <code>packages</code>, <code>experiments</code>, <code>papers</code>, <code>books</code>, <code>talks</code> or <code>projects</code>, and each key in it a directory of that group. Each directory is its key in title case below the research root.</p><p>Copy the file to <code>repositories.toml</code> beside the profile, or name it with <code>$RESEARCH_HARNESS_REPOSITORIES</code>, and write your repositories into the copy. The file is optional: without it, <code>harness repos</code> takes each git repository that it finds, and <code>harness clone</code> stops. The contract sweep writes a table of its own into its fixture.</p><h2 id="project-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>Project.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`Project.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Julia environment of the scripts of the harness. It names the eight packages that the scripts load beyond the standard library, with their <code>[compat]</code> bounds: Aqua, ExplicitImports, JET, JSON, JuliaFormatter, JuliaSyntax, TestEnv and YAML.</p><p><code>harness install --apply</code> copies the file to <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/julia/</code>, or to <code>$RESEARCH_HARNESS_JULIA</code>, and instantiates it there. Each script that loads one of the packages puts that environment on its load path. No <code>Manifest.toml</code> is committed.</p><p>The scripts never use this file in place. A session can edit the checkout, and the update job runs outside the sandbox, so the job must install only the packages that the installed copy names. <a href="./../dependencies#aqua-explicitimports-jet-json-juliaformatter-juliasyntax-testenv-and-yaml">Dependencies</a> says what each package does.</p><h2 id="fatou-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>fatou.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#fatou-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`fatou.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The global configuration of fatou, a linter for Julia code. fatou reads <code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fatou/fatou.toml</code>, and does not look above the root of a git repository. Copy the file by hand, and copy it again after a change:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">cp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/Research/Harness/fatou.toml</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/.config/fatou/fatou.toml</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The file turns off the rule <code>unused-import</code>. That rule does not follow <code>include</code>, so in a package whose imports are in the module file, almost every finding is false. ExplicitImports answers that question instead.</p><p>The installed pre-commit hook runs <code>fatou lint</code> with this configuration, and prints its findings as advice. <code>scripts/mutate.jl</code> writes a configuration of its own and does not read this file.</p><h2 id="research-sgconfig-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>research-sgconfig.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#research-sgconfig-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`research-sgconfig.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The configuration of ast-grep, a tool that searches code by its syntax tree, for the whole research tree. Copy it by hand to <code>~/Research/sgconfig.yml</code>. ast-grep looks for <code>sgconfig.yml</code> in each parent directory, so the file applies to every repository of the tree.</p><p>Julia is not a language that ast-grep knows. The file registers <code>julia</code> as a custom language for <code>.jl</code> files, with the parser library <code>Harness/ast-grep/julia.dylib</code>, which <code>build-julia.sh</code> builds. It also names <code>Harness/ast-grep/rules</code> as the directory of the rules and <code>Harness/ast-grep/rule-tests</code> as the directory of the rule tests. The paths are relative to <code>~/Research</code>, so the checkout must be at <code>~/Research/Harness</code>.</p><h2 id="build-julia-sh" tabindex="-1"><code>build-julia.sh</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#build-julia-sh" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`build-julia.sh`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A bash script that builds the Julia parser library of ast-grep. It fetches the tree-sitter-julia grammar at a pinned commit into a temporary directory. Then it compiles the grammar with <code>cc</code> into <code>julia.dylib</code> beside the script, and deletes the temporary directory.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ast-grep/build-julia.sh</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Run it once on each machine, and again after a change of the pinned commit. <code>julia.dylib</code> is ignored by git, so it is never committed. <code>research-sgconfig.yml</code> names the library.</p><h2 id="rules-parse-error-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>rules/parse-error.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-parse-error-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rules/parse-error.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A rule of ast-grep for Julia code. It marks each place where tree-sitter-julia could not parse the code: a node of the kind <code>ERROR</code>. Its severity is <code>info</code>.</p><p>The rule tells you where a search with a pattern can miss code, because the syntax tree there is not correct. <code>research-sgconfig.yml</code> names <code>ast-grep/rules/</code> as the directory of the rules, so ast-grep uses the rule in every repository of the tree.</p><h2 id="the-rules-of-severity-error" tabindex="-1">The rules of severity error <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rules-of-severity-error" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rules of severity error&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The five rules in the sections below have the severity <code>error</code>. Each one finds a defect in Julia code that a pattern over the syntax tree decides with a low risk of a false hit. The message of each rule names the fix. To silence one hit, write the comment <code># ast-grep-ignore: &lt;rule-id&gt;</code> at the end of the line or on the line above. Two rules match a comment, and for them this works only in part: <code>docstring-comment</code> cannot be silenced, and <code>line-citation-comment</code> is silenced only from the line above.</p><h2 id="rules-docstring-comment-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>rules/docstring-comment.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-docstring-comment-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rules/docstring-comment.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The rule <code>docstring-comment</code> finds a <code>#</code> comment directly after the closing <code>&quot;&quot;&quot;</code> of a docstring, before its definition. Such a comment detaches the docstring: the definition has no docstring, and each <code>@ref</code> to it fails. Move the comment above the docstring or into the definition.</p><p>The rule matches a comment that is the next sibling of a triple-quoted string. It does not match a comment after a one-line <code>&quot;…&quot;</code> string, because a plain string statement has the same shape. It also does not match a comment inside the definition, or a comment at the end of the definition&#39;s line.</p><p>The rule cannot be silenced. A comment <code># ast-grep-ignore: docstring-comment</code> after the docstring is itself a hit, at the end of the <code>&quot;&quot;&quot;</code> line too. A comment in this place always detaches the docstring, so the fix is to move it.</p><h2 id="rules-allocated-equality-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>rules/allocated-equality.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-allocated-equality-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rules/allocated-equality.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The rule <code>allocated-equality</code> finds two <code>@allocated</code> readings compared exactly: <code>@allocated(a) == @allocated(b)</code> or <code>@allocated(a) === @allocated(b)</code>, inside <code>@test</code> too. A reading in parentheses, such as <code>(@allocated a)</code>, counts too. Windows quantises the figures by 16 bytes, so an exact comparison fails there. Assert the difference under a stated tolerance instead. A comparison of one reading with a number, or with <code>&lt;=</code>, is not a hit.</p><h2 id="rules-exitcode-alone-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>rules/exitcode-alone.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-exitcode-alone-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rules/exitcode-alone.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The rule <code>exitcode-alone</code> finds <code>p.exitcode</code> in a definition that neither reads <code>termsignal</code> nor calls <code>success</code>. A process that a signal ends has the exit code 0 in Julia, so the exit code alone reads it as a success. Check <code>termsignal</code> too, or call <code>success(p)</code>.</p><p>A definition is a <code>function … end</code> or a short form <code>f(…) = …</code>, also with a return type or a <code>where</code> clause, as in <code>f(p)::Bool = …</code>. The rule looks for <code>termsignal</code> and a call of <code>success</code>, also as <code>Base.success(p)</code>, in the enclosing definition, and for a read at top level in the whole file. A <code>termsignal</code> read in another function does not count.</p><h2 id="rules-line-citation-comment-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>rules/line-citation-comment.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-line-citation-comment-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rules/line-citation-comment.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The rule <code>line-citation-comment</code> finds a comment that holds a file name ending in <code>.jl</code>, a colon and digits, such as <code>solver.jl:120</code>. The next edit that changes the line count of that file makes the citation false. Name the function instead. A ratio such as <code>3:1</code>, a file name without a line, and the same text in a string are not hits.</p><p>A comment <code># ast-grep-ignore: line-citation-comment</code> at the end of the line is part of the same comment, so it does not silence the hit. Write it on the line above.</p><h2 id="rules-debug-probe-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>rules/debug-probe.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-debug-probe-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rules/debug-probe.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The rule <code>debug-probe</code> finds a temporary probe in a file under <code>src/</code> or <code>ext/</code>: an <code>@show</code> call, <code>Base.@show</code> too, or a string that holds <code>[DEBUG-</code>. Remove the probe before the commit. Tests, scripts and docs may print, so the rule has the <code>files:</code> globs <code>**/src/**/*.jl</code> and <code>**/ext/**/*.jl</code>.</p><h2 id="the-rule-tests" tabindex="-1">The rule tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rule-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rule tests&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The directory <code>rule-tests/</code> holds the rule tests of ast-grep: one file <code>&lt;rule-id&gt;-test.yml</code> for each rule of severity <code>error</code>. Each file holds <code>valid</code> snippets, which the rule must not match, and <code>invalid</code> snippets, which it must match. <code>ast-grep test</code> runs them:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ast-grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> test</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -c</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/Research/sgconfig.yml</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --custom-languages</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> allow</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --skip-snapshot-tests</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Without <code>--custom-languages allow</code>, ast-grep ignores the language <code>julia</code>, and each test that expects no hit passes. A snippet has no path, so a rule test cannot test the <code>files:</code> globs of <code>debug-probe</code>. The directory <code>rule-tests/scope/</code> holds the file <code>probe.jl</code>, with one <code>@show x</code>, under <code>src/</code>, <code>ext/</code>, <code>test/</code> and <code>scripts/</code>. A scan of <code>scope/</code> with <code>debug-probe</code> must hit exactly <code>src/probe.jl</code> and <code>ext/probe.jl</code>.</p><h3 id="the-ast-grep-job-of-ci" tabindex="-1">The <code>ast-grep</code> job of CI <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ast-grep-job-of-ci" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The `ast-grep` job of CI&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The job <code>ast-grep</code> of the workflow <code>julia.yml</code> runs the rule tests. The path filter of the workflow names <code>ast-grep/**</code>, so a change of a rule or a rule test alone starts it. The job installs the release 0.51.1 of ast-grep, with its SHA-256, and builds the parser with <code>build-julia.sh</code>. <code>research-sgconfig.yml</code> names its paths relative to <code>~/Research</code>, so the job writes a configuration with the checkout&#39;s absolute paths. Then it runs <code>ast-grep test</code> and the scan of <code>rule-tests/scope/</code>. The job fails when a test fails, when a test names a rule that does not exist, or when the scan does not hit exactly <code>src/probe.jl</code> and <code>ext/probe.jl</code>.</p><h2 id="gitleaks-toml" tabindex="-1"><code>.gitleaks.toml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#gitleaks-toml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`.gitleaks.toml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The configuration of gitleaks, the tool that finds secrets in text. The pre-push hook of this repository and the <code>leaks</code> job of CI read it.</p><p>It keeps the default rules of gitleaks, and adds the rule <code>home-path</code>: a path below <code>/Users/</code> or <code>/home/</code> that names a user. The rule allows only the two example homes of the repository, <code>/Users/me</code> and <code>/home/example</code>. Every call of gitleaks passes <code>--ignore-gitleaks-allow</code>, so a <code>gitleaks:allow</code> comment has no effect.</p><p>Remove a false positive by a change of the text, not of the configuration. A new allowlist entry needs the approval of the user and its own line in <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>. <code>harness leaks</code> finds the private strings of the profile, which gitleaks does not know. <a href="./../development">Development</a> describes both checks.</p><h2 id="tests-skill-triggers" tabindex="-1"><code>tests/skill-triggers</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#tests-skill-triggers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`tests/skill-triggers`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The queries of the skill-trigger test, one TOML file for each skill of <code>skills/</code>, <code>tests/skill-triggers/&lt;skill&gt;.toml</code>. <code>harness skill-triggers</code> reads them.</p><p>A skill that the model can load has <code>load</code>, 10 queries that must load it, and <code>near</code>, 5 queries that must not. Each <code>near</code> entry names the skill that the query belongs to, or <code>none</code>. A skill with <code>disable-model-invocation: true</code> has one <code>near</code> query and no <code>load</code>. A query must not start with <code>/</code>, and must not occur twice in one file. A <code>near</code> entry must not name the skill of its own file. On another count or form, <code>harness skill-triggers</code> exits with status 2.</p><p><code>fixtures/</code> holds recorded <code>stream-json</code> output of Claude Code sessions, with the private content removed, and some streams written by hand. The cases of <code>harness test</code> read them to check how a session&#39;s output is judged.</p><p>Run the test after you change the <code>description</code> of a skill. <a href="./../daily-use">Typical use</a> shows the commands.</p>',104)])])}const m=o(d,[["render",c]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
